- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Presidential pardon committee finalizes measures to release 30 prisoners","publisher":"State Information Service","publication_date":"2022-11-15","url":"https://sis.gov.eg/en/media-center/news/presidential-pardon-committee-finalizes-measures-to-release-30-prisoners/","claim":"The presidential pardon committee said it finalized measures to release 30 prisoners held in pre-trial detention and would keep working with prosecution and Interior to release more detainees.","locator":"Lead paragraph","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official statement on a specific detainee-release action in 2022.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"target-year 2022 remedial action","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Sisi-backed pardon mechanism and state prosecutorial process.","contrary_evidence":["The release of 30 people is modest relative to the scale of reported arbitrary detention."],"lenses":["3B.4","3B.6","3B.8","3B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Sisi declares 2022 as year of civil society","publisher":"State Information Service","publication_date":"2022-01-14","url":"https://sis.gov.eg/en/media-center/news/sisi-declares-2022-as-year-of-civil-society/","claim":"Sisi publicly declared 2022 the year of civil society and urged state bodies to create a dialogue platform with local and international NGOs.","locator":"Lead paragraph","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary presidential statement, useful as favorable rhetoric and stated intent.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"target-year 2022 policy signal","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Sisi.","contrary_evidence":["Rights reporting shows that the rhetoric did not prevent arrests, travel bans, and pressure on civil society during 2022."],"lenses":["3B.4","3B.8","3B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Egypt reforms prisons and highlights rights, in moves critics call hollow","publisher":"Reuters via MarketScreener","publication_date":"2022-01-27","url":"https://www.marketscreener.com/news/latest/Egypt-reforms-prisons-and-highlights-rights-in-moves-critics-call-hollow-37655897/","claim":"Reuters reported that Egypt showcased prison reforms, a five-year human rights strategy, a new rights council, and the lifting of the state of emergency, while critics called the steps superficial and said political prisoners remained detained.","locator":"Lead paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent wire reporting that captures both official claims and critical rebuttal.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"target-year 2022 reform narrative","ruler_attribution":"State-level reform posture under Sisi, with clear limits on practical effect.","contrary_evidence":["The same article quotes critics who say the reforms are cosmetic and do not improve detainee treatment."],"lenses":["3B.4","3B.5","3B.6","3B.8","3B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
- I screened roughly 30 documents across Egyptian official pages, UN/IGO material, U.S. and NGO country reports, Reuters mirrors, and local/regional reporting.
- I retained a mixed set that covers the core 2022 domestic-safety story: police torture, death in custody, COP27 repression, prison conditions, arbitrary detention, women’s safety, refugees, LGBTQI+ abuse, and a small amount of remedial/pardon evidence.
- I rejected duplicates where multiple publishers repeated the same incident without adding a materially different claim, especially around the COP27 detentions and the Ayman Hadhoud case.
- I rejected broad background explainers that repeated older Egypt repression history without adding a 2022-specific fact.
- I rejected several advocacy or commentary items that were too generic, not Egypt-specific, or not tied to a ruler-attributable safety decision.
- I also set aside some out-of-period material that only served as background when the 2022 evidence was already sufficient.
- A few direct-site fetches were blocked or noisy, so I used reliable mirrors or open-page searches where the primary page was not accessible.

**Remaining gaps**
- The evidence is strong on recurring and at times widespread repression, but weaker on the exact chain of command from Sisi personally to every police/prison decision.
- The record is better for state security and detention abuse than for granular civilian-protection policy design.
- The positive record is limited to rhetorical reform signals and a handful of pardons/releases; it does not show systemic remedy.
- More search would help only if you want narrower proof on accountability outcomes for specific abusive units, or a more granular measure of whether the pardon/release initiatives materially reduced the detention pool in 2022.
